FEED ENTRY — ONE-ON-ONE
Date extracted: April 12, 2026
Transcript type: One-on-one
Client type: Composer and teacher, mid-career, balancing heavy teaching load and family commitments, based in Sweden

Primary block: Creative work continuously sidelined by teaching schedule and family responsibilities. Client described Thursday and Friday as protected time but acknowledged those days had defaulted to recovery and housekeeping rather than artistic work. “It’s become kind of a habit to kind of protect this time that I’m not teaching — it’s the time to breathe or to do the housekeeping. But then the other stuff gets pushed to the background.”

Identity signal: Not strongly present. Client did not question whether they could still do the work — the piece exists, the vision is clear. The block is structural and behavioral, not identity-level.

Turning point: Coach issued a live challenge — find the contact email for a local guitar duo and send an inquiry email during the call. Client located the email, drafted the message in Swedish with coach input, and sent it before the call ended. Coach’s framing: “If we do nothing else in this call, I think getting this project in motion is worth it.” Client’s response after sending: “It feels good.”

Language that landed:
— “Unless you’re just like, yeah, about it, it’ll stay on the back burner for another three months.” (Direct naming of the avoidance pattern; client responded with visible recognition: “Yeah, exactly. Wow.”)
— “I don’t rely much on mindset. I rely on habits and cues and environment.” (Reframe that shifted the conversation from motivation to physical design.)
— “Paint yourself into a corner” — framing studio booking as a commitment mechanism before the player is confirmed.

Recurring pattern: Possible. “I’ll do it when I feel ready / when conditions are right” as a delay structure appears across multiple client threads. The insight that excitement must be present or the project will stall is consistent territory.

Intervention that worked: Live action during the session. Rather than assigning the email as a next step, coach challenged client to send it on the call. Removed the gap between session and execution. Client went from “yeah, I could reach out to them” to email sent in under ten minutes. Physical habit cue reinforced through the same mechanism — coach asked client to physically retrieve the “musician backpack” and bring it on camera during the call, making the Wednesday-night packing ritual concrete and present rather than abstract.

END ENTRY​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​
